: theorem @=thm:undecidability
  Tiling $\Z^3$ by translations of a single polycube is co-RE-complete,
  and hence undecidable.

***Co-RE*** is the complexity class of decision problems whose "no" instances
can be recognized in finite time by an algorithm (Turing machine).
A problem is ***co-RE-hard*** if every co-RE problem can be reduced to it
via a computable many-one reduction,
and ***co-RE-complete*** if it is also in co-RE.
It is known that some co-RE-complete problems
(such as "does a given algorithm run forever?")\ are undecidable,
so co-RE-completeness implies undecidability.

We prove co-RE-hardness by reducing from the problem of Wang tiling,
introduced by [[Wang]] and proved co-RE-hard by [[Berger]].
A ***Wang tile*** is a unit square with a color on each of its four edges.
Given a set $W$ of Wang tiles,
a ***Wang tiling*** assigns a tile from $W$ to each grid point in $\Z^2$
such that, when placing those tiles by translation,
touching edges have the same color.
Deciding whether a given finite set of Wang tiles admits such a tiling
is co-RE-complete [[Berger]].

Thus our main task is to construct a computable map from a
finite nonempty Wang tile set $\calT$
to a finite nonempty connected set $F_{\calT}\subset\Z^3$ such that
  $$
   \calT\text{ tiles }\Z^2
   \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
   F_{\calT}\text{ tiles }\Z^3\text{ by translations}.
  $$

---

For the purposes of this paper, define a ***prototile*** in $d$ dimensions to
be a ***polycube***: a set of points in $\Z^d$, or equivalently
a finite union of unit cubes in $\R^d$.


Following the tiling-equation notation of
\cite[Section~1.1]{GTUndecidable}, for subsets $A,F$ of an abelian
group $G$, the notation $A\oplus F=G$
means that every element of $G$ has exactly one representation as $a+f$
with $a\in A$ and $f\in F$.  We say that $F$ ***tiles*** $G$ if such an $A$
exists.  The input ***tile*** is finite and nonempty; the translation set may
be infinite.  We allow translations only.  Intermediate tiles may be
disconnected; the final tile will be connected.
A finite set $F\subset\Z^d$ is ***connected*** if every pair of its
points can be joined by a sequence of points of $F$, successive points
differing by a signed coordinate unit vector.
Connectedness of polycubes and polyominoes always refers to this lattice
adjacency, including when they are viewed as unions of unit cubes.
A ***polycube*** is a finite nonempty subset of $\Z^3$, or its corresponding
union of unit cubes; a ***polyomino*** is its two-dimensional counterpart,
following the terminology of \cite[Section~1.2]{Kim}.
A tiling using one tile type is a ***translational monotiling***.
More generally, $B\oplus C=E$ denotes unique representation of every
point of $E$ as a sum, with no sums outside $E$; $B+C$ denotes the set
of sums without a uniqueness condition, and $B-C$ is the set of
differences $b-c$.  For a vector $u$ and an integer $n$, write
$u+B=\{u+b:b\in B\}$ and $nB=\{nb:b\in B\}$.
The notation $\sqcup$ denotes a disjoint union.
A ***simultaneous tiling system*** asks for one translation
set $A$ satisfying $A\oplus F_\nu=G$ for every specified tile $F_\nu$,
as in \cite[Section~3]{GTPeriodic}.

For a finite nonempty $F\subset\Z^d$, its ***unit-cube union*** is
$F+[0,1]^d$.  In $\R^d$, a ***tiling by translations*** means that each point
belongs to exactly one translated copy, except on a set of Lebesgue
measure zero; this is coverage with multiplicity one almost everywhere
\cite[Section~1.1]{GTPeriodic}.


: theorem @=thm:main

Replacing each lattice point by a unit cube gives co-RE-completeness
for deciding whether a single connected polycube tiles $\R^3$ by arbitrary real translations.

The reduction combines four constructions.  A ***Sudoku array*** has
finitely many columns and integer-indexed rows, where every integer-slope
line must read as one of a specified finite set of words.
Greenfeld and Tao encode Wang tilings using such arrays, with two
arithmetic scales providing the coordinates of the simulated plane
\cite[Section~5]{GTUndecidable}.  OpenAI's preprint encodes a one-prime
Sudoku obstruction by simultaneous tiling equations with a cyclic
finite factor \cite[Sections~3--5]{OpenAI2026}.  We combine these
equations into one tile while keeping the finite factor cyclic
\cite[Section~6]{OpenAI2026}.  An effective quotient reduction preserves
the number of tiles when passing from a quotient of $\Z^3$ to $\Z^3$
\cite[Theorem~1.1]{MSS}.
Kim's reduction then makes the tile connected while preserving
dimension and tileability \cite[Theorem~2.5]{Kim}.

Our modification of the cyclic encoding replaces each arithmetic symbol
by a pair of arithmetic symbols and a Wang label.  Two auxiliary
constraints restrict only the arithmetic coordinates and force both
components to vary in every column.  A Wang tiling supplies an array
that supports both constraints simultaneously.  We include the
finite-group construction and its inverse calculations to verify this compatibility; constructing
separate solutions of the different tiling equations would not suffice.
\Cref{fig:reduction} summarizes the existence equivalences.

: figure[tbp]
  \centering
  \begin{tikzpicture}[
    font=\small, >=Latex,
    stage/.style={draw=black!55, rounded corners=2pt, fill=black!3,
      text width=4.0cm, minimum height=1.05cm, align=center},
    reductionarrow/.style={->, semithick, draw=black!65}]
    \node[stage] (wang) at (0,0) {Wang tiling\\of the plane};
    \node[stage] (sudoku) at (5.3,0) {Decorated Sudoku array\\arithmetic components\\nonconstant in each column};
    \node[stage] (system) at (10.6,0) {Simultaneous tiling equations\\with a cyclic finite factor};
    \node[stage] (stacked) at (0,-2) {One tile in $\Z^2$\\with a cyclic finite factor};
    \node[stage] (lattice) at (5.3,-2) {One finite lattice tile\\in $\Z^3$};
    \node[stage] (connected) at (10.6,-2) {One connected tile\\in $\Z^3$};
    \draw[reductionarrow] (wang) -- (sudoku);
    \draw[reductionarrow] (sudoku) -- (system);
    \draw[reductionarrow,rounded corners=4pt]
      (system.east) -- (13.2,0) -- (13.2,-1)
      -- (-2.6,-1) -- (-2.6,-2) -- (stacked.west);
    \draw[reductionarrow] (stacked) -- (lattice);
    \draw[reductionarrow] (lattice) -- (connected);
  \end{tikzpicture}
  \caption{The reduction, read left to right in each row, with the
    connecting arrow continuing from the top row to the bottom row.
    Arrows indicate the direction of the reduction; every step preserves
    existence of a solution in both directions. The Sudoku interface is from
    \cite[Section~5]{GTUndecidable}; the cyclic encoding adapts
    \cite[Sections~3--6]{OpenAI2026}; the lattice and connectedness steps
    use \cite[Theorem~1.1]{MSS} and \cite[Theorem~2.5]{Kim}, respectively.}
  \label{fig:reduction}

.# Scope of the Ingredients.
The reduction in \cite{GTUndecidable} allows a variable finite abelian
factor and a periodic target subset.  Its lattice dimension is part of
the input; that result alone does not give \cref{thm:main}.
Here the finite factor is cyclic and the target is the whole group.
For the quotient step we use \cite{MSS}, which preserves arbitrary tilings.
The periodic-tiling descent in \cite[Section~7]{OpenAI2026} alone would
not justify an undecidability reduction.

